/* Verse Games: THE WHOLE COUNSEL · Ezekiel chapters 26-30
   Verse text is filled/verified from the KJV by tools/fill-verses.js */
VG.registerBibleChapters("ezekiel", [
{n:26, name:"The Doom of Tyre", seal:{ref:"Ezekiel 26:5",verse:"It shall be a place for the spreading of nets in the midst of the sea: for I have spoken it, saith the Lord GOD: and it shall become a spoil to the nations."}, facts:["Did you know the fall of Tyre begins with a sneer? When Jerusalem fell, Tyre gloated, 'Aha, I shall be replenished,' and God answered that gloating with wave upon wave of nations (Ezekiel 26:2 and 3).","Did you know Ezekiel said Tyre would become a place for the spreading of nets? Centuries later Alexander the Great scraped the old city's ruins into the sea to build a causeway, leaving the bare rock to fishermen (Ezekiel 26:5 and 14).","Did you know Nebuchadrezzar besieged Tyre for thirteen years? Ezekiel names him a king of kings from the north, and history records one of the longest sieges of the ancient world (Ezekiel 26:7)."], questions:[
{q:"When did the word of the LORD come to Ezekiel against Tyre?", options:["In the eleventh year, in the first day of the month","In the tenth year, in the tenth month","In the twelfth year, in the fifth day of the month","In the eleventh year, in the seventh month"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 26:1",verse:"And it came to pass in the eleventh year, in the first day of the month, that the word of the LORD came unto me, saying,", insight:"God dates His word; judgment does not fall on a whim but on a set day."},
{q:"What had Tyrus said against Jerusalem?", options:["Aha, she is broken; she is turned unto me: I shall be replenished, now she is laid waste","Blessed be Jerusalem, the city of peace","We will rebuild her walls together","Jerusalem shall never fall"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 26:2",verse:"Son of man, because that Tyrus hath said against Jerusalem, Aha, she is broken that was the gates of the people: she is turned unto me: I shall be replenished, now she is laid waste:", insight:"Gloating over a neighbor's ruin invites God's answer; pity builds, mockery is answered."},
{q:"What will God cause to come up against Tyrus?", options:["Many nations, as the sea causeth his waves to come up","A single army from the east","A flood that drowns the city","Fire from heaven"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 26:3",verse:"Therefore thus saith the Lord GOD; Behold, I am against thee, O Tyrus, and will cause many nations to come up against thee, as the sea causeth his waves to come up.", insight:"Wave upon wave of nations; God's judgment keeps coming like the tide."},
{q:"What will the nations do to the walls of Tyrus?", options:["Destroy her walls and break down her towers","Rebuild them higher than before","Set them on fire at night","Leave them standing as a monument"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 26:4",verse:"And they shall destroy the walls of Tyrus, and break down her towers: I will also scrape her dust from her, and make her like the top of a rock.", insight:"Even the strongest walls are sand before God's sentence."},
{q:"What will Tyrus become in the midst of the sea?", options:["A place for the spreading of nets","A harbor for all nations","A fortress of iron","A garden of cedars"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 26:5",verse:"It shall be a place for the spreading of nets in the midst of the sea: for I have spoken it, saith the Lord GOD: and it shall become a spoil to the nations.", insight:"A trading empire reduced to a drying rack for nets; pride's end is always smaller than its boast."},
{q:"What will happen to her daughters in the field?", options:["They shall be slain by the sword","They shall be carried to Babylon","They shall become merchants","They shall flee to the isles"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 26:6",verse:"And her daughters which are in the field shall be slain by the sword; and they shall know that I am the LORD.", insight:"The suburbs share the city's fate; no one stands apart from the judgment on the whole."},
{q:"Whom will God bring upon Tyrus?", options:["Nebuchadrezzar king of Babylon, a king of kings, from the north","The king of Egypt with his chariots","The princes of the sea","The army of Persia"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 26:7",verse:"For thus saith the Lord GOD; Behold, I will bring upon Tyrus Nebuchadrezzar king of Babylon, a king of kings, from the north, with horses, and with chariots, and with horsemen, and companies, and much people.", insight:"God calls a pagan king a king of kings and puts him on a leash; even empires serve His purposes."},
{q:"What will Nebuchadrezzar make against Tyrus?", options:["A fort, a mount, and lift up the buckler","A wall of brass around it","A trench filled with water","A tower to the heavens"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 26:8",verse:"He shall slay with the sword thy daughters in the field: and he shall make a fort against thee, and cast a mount against thee, and lift up the buckler against thee.", insight:"Siege works against a proud city; God engineers the downfall of every fortress."},
{q:"With what will he break down her towers?", options:["With engines of war and with his axes","With battering rams of gold","With fire and brimstone","With the hands of his soldiers"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 26:9",verse:"And he shall set engines of war against thy walls, and with his axes he shall break down thy towers.", insight:"Towers built by pride fall to axes in God's hand."},
{q:"By what will Tyrus be covered when the horsemen enter?", options:["The dust of the abundance of his horses","A cloud of locusts","The smoke of burning timber","The ashes of her own fires"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 26:10",verse:"By reason of the abundance of his horses their dust shall cover thee: thy walls shall shake at the noise of the horsemen, and of the wheels, and of the chariots, when he shall enter into thy gates, as men enter into a city wherein is made a breach.", insight:"The dust of hooves covers a city that once glittered; judgment dims every glory."},
{q:"What will the hoofs of his horses tread down?", options:["All thy streets","The gates of the sea","The walls of the city","The ships in the harbor"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 26:11",verse:"With the hoofs of his horses shall he tread down all thy streets: he shall slay thy people by the sword, and thy strong garrisons shall go down to the ground.", insight:"Streets that rang with trade will ring with hooves; what we fill with pride, God empties."},
{q:"Where will her stones, timber, and dust be laid?", options:["In the midst of the water","On the top of the mountain","In the fields of the daughters","Before the gates of Babylon"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 26:12",verse:"And they shall make a spoil of thy riches, and make a prey of thy merchandise: and they shall break down thy walls, and destroy thy pleasant houses: and they shall lay thy stones and thy timber and thy dust in the midst of the water.", insight:"The city's very stones thrown into the sea; God unbuilds what pride erected."},
{q:"What music will God silence in Tyrus?", options:["The noise of her songs and the sound of her harps","The trumpets of her army","The flutes of her temples","The drums of her markets"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 26:13",verse:"And I will cause the noise of thy songs to cease; and the sound of thy harps shall be no more heard.", insight:"When the songs stop, the party is over; judgment silences the music of self praise."},
{q:"How long will Tyrus lie unbuilt?", options:["She shall be built no more","For forty years","Until the third generation","For a thousand years"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 26:14",verse:"And I will make thee like the top of a rock: thou shalt be a place to spread nets upon; thou shalt be built no more: for I the LORD have spoken it, saith the Lord GOD.", insight:"I the LORD have spoken it; when God says no more, no more it is."},
{q:"What will shake at the sound of Tyre's fall?", options:["The isles","The mountains of Israel","The walls of Babylon","The gates of Egypt"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 26:15",verse:"Thus saith the Lord GOD to Tyrus; Shall not the isles shake at the sound of thy fall, when the wounded cry, when the slaughter is made in the midst of thee?", insight:"When great cities fall, the whole neighborhood trembles; pride's crash echoes far."},
{q:"What will the princes of the sea do at Tyre's fall?", options:["Come down from their thrones, lay away their robes, and clothe themselves with trembling","Sail away in their ships","Send tribute to Babylon","Build new thrones of ivory"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 26:16",verse:"Then all the princes of the sea shall come down from their thrones, and lay away their robes, and put off their broidered garments: they shall clothe themselves with trembling; they shall sit upon the ground, and shall tremble at every moment, and be astonished at thee.", insight:"Kings step down when God's judgment steps in; trembling is the wise response."},
{q:"In their lamentation, what do they call Tyre?", options:["The renowned city, inhabited of seafaring men","The queen of the desert","The mother of all ships","The crown of the east"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 26:17",verse:"And they shall take up a lamentation for thee, and say to thee, How art thou destroyed, that wast inhabited of seafaring men, the renowned city, which wast strong in the sea, she and her inhabitants, which cause their terror to be on all that haunt it!", insight:"Even her mourners remember her glory; lamentation admits what pride denied."},
{q:"What will God bring up upon Tyrus?", options:["The deep, and great waters shall cover her","A host of locusts","A famine of seven years","A plague of darkness"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 26:19",verse:"For thus saith the Lord GOD; When I shall make thee a desolate city, like the cities that are not inhabited; when I shall bring up the deep upon thee, and great waters shall cover thee;", insight:"The sea that made her rich will cover her; the source of pride becomes the instrument of judgment."},
{q:"Where will God set Tyrus?", options:["In the low parts of the earth, with them that go down to the pit","On the holy mountain of God","Among the cities of the living","In the midst of the nations"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 26:20",verse:"When I shall bring thee down with them that descend into the pit, with the people of old time, and shall set thee in the low parts of the earth, in places desolate of old, with them that go down to the pit, that thou be not inhabited; and I shall set glory in the land of the living;", insight:"Down to the pit with the people of old time; every exalted city has an appointment with dust."},
{q:"What is Tyre's final sentence?", options:["Thou shalt be no more; though thou be sought for, yet shalt thou never be found again","She shall rise again in glory","She shall be rebuilt by her daughters","She shall rule the isles forever"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 26:21",verse:"I will make thee a terror, and thou shalt be no more: though thou be sought for, yet shalt thou never be found again, saith the Lord GOD.", insight:"Sought for and never found; that is the epitaph of pride."}
]},
{n:27, name:"The Ship of Tyre", seal:{ref:"Ezekiel 27:26",verse:"Thy rowers have brought thee into great waters: the east wind hath broken thee in the midst of the seas."}, facts:["Did you know Ezekiel's lament for Tyre names her trading partners like a world atlas? Tarshish in Spain, Javan in Greece, Togarmah in Asia Minor, Sheba in Arabia, and Judah trading wheat, honey, oil, and balm (Ezekiel 27:12, 13, 17, and 22).","Did you know the whole lament builds Tyre as a perfect ship, plank by plank, and then breaks her with a single east wind? The proudest vessel ever launched in Scripture sinks in one verse (Ezekiel 27:4 through 7, and 26).","Did you know the Gammadims who stood in Tyre's towers are named nowhere else in Scripture? Scholars take them for elite guards or watchmen posted on her walls (Ezekiel 27:11)."], questions:[
{q:"What is Ezekiel told to take up for Tyrus?", options:["A lamentation","A song of praise","A covenant","A warning to flee"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 27:2",verse:"Now, thou son of man, take up a lamentation for Tyrus;", insight:"God weeps over the cities He judges; lamentation is love's last word to the proud."},
{q:"How is Tyrus described at the entry of the sea?", options:["A merchant of the people for many isles","A fortress of the north","A city of the wilderness","A haven for the poor"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 27:3",verse:"And say unto Tyrus, O thou that art situate at the entry of the sea, which art a merchant of the people for many isles, Thus saith the Lord GOD; O Tyrus, thou hast said, I am of perfect beauty.", insight:"Trade made her great and trade made her proud; blessing without gratitude curdles."},
{q:"What proud claim had Tyrus made?", options:["I am of perfect beauty","I am a god forever","No king shall enter me","My walls reach heaven"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 27:3",verse:"And say unto Tyrus, O thou that art situate at the entry of the sea, which art a merchant of the people for many isles, Thus saith the Lord GOD; O Tyrus, thou hast said, I am of perfect beauty.", insight:"Perfect beauty in her own mirror; self praise is the fog before the fall."},
{q:"Of what were her ship boards made?", options:["Fir trees of Senir, with masts of cedars from Lebanon","Oak from Bashan","Palm from Egypt","Teak from the east"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 27:5",verse:"They have made all thy ship boards of fir trees of Senir: they have taken cedars from Lebanon to make masts for thee.", insight:"The finest timber in the world built her ships; the best materials cannot save a doomed voyage."},
{q:"Of what were her oars made?", options:["The oaks of Bashan","The cedars of Lebanon","The firs of Senir","The pines of Arvad"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 27:6",verse:"Of the oaks of Bashan have they made thine oars; the company of the Ashurites have made thy benches of ivory, brought out of the isles of Chittim.", insight:"Every plank named, every timber known; God catalogs the glory He is about to break."},
{q:"What was her sail, and what covered her?", options:["Fine linen with broidered work from Egypt for her sail; blue and purple from the isles of Elishah for her covering","Purple silk from Tyre for both","Goatskin sails and tarred canvas","White wool and scarlet cloth"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 27:7",verse:"Fine linen with broidered work from Egypt was that which thou spreadest forth to be thy sail; blue and purple from the isles of Elishah was that which covered thee.", insight:"Egypt's linen and Elishah's purple; the world dressed her, and the world will watch her sink."},
{q:"Who were her mariners and her pilots?", options:["The inhabitants of Zidon and Arvad were her mariners; her own wise men were her pilots","Persians and Medes","Egyptians and Ethiopians","Greeks and Romans"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 27:8",verse:"The inhabitants of Zidon and Arvad were thy mariners: thy wise men, O Tyrus, that were in thee, were thy pilots.", insight:"The wisest pilots in the world, and still the ship goes down; wisdom without God steers toward the rocks."},
{q:"Who were her calkers?", options:["The ancients of Gebal and the wise men thereof","The shipwrights of Tarshish","The carpenters of Javan","The builders of Zidon"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 27:9",verse:"The ancients of Gebal and the wise men thereof were in thee thy calkers: all the ships of the sea with their mariners were in thee to occupy thy merchandise.", insight:"Calkers seal the seams, but no caulking holds against the east wind of God."},
{q:"Who served as her men of war?", options:["They of Persia and of Lud and of Phut","The men of Arvad","The Gammadims","The princes of Kedar"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 27:10",verse:"They of Persia and of Lud and of Phut were in thine army, thy men of war: they hanged the shield and helmet in thee; they set forth thy comeliness.", insight:"Mercenaries from three nations hung shield and helmet in her; hired swords cannot defend a judged city."},
{q:"Who stood in her towers?", options:["The Gammadims","The men of Persia","The ancients of Gebal","The pilots of the sea"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 27:11",verse:"The men of Arvad with thine army were upon thy walls round about, and the Gammadims were in thy towers: they hanged their shields upon thy walls round about; they have made thy beauty perfect.", insight:"Guards in every tower, and still the city falls; walls cannot keep out the word of the LORD."},
{q:"What did Tarshish trade in her fairs?", options:["Silver, iron, tin, and lead","Gold and precious stones","Spices and ivory","Horses and mules"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 27:12",verse:"Tarshish was thy merchant by reason of the multitude of all kind of riches; with silver, iron, tin, and lead, they traded in thy fairs.", insight:"Silver, iron, tin, and lead; the metals of the world flowed to her markets like rivers."},
{q:"What did Javan, Tubal, and Meshech trade?", options:["The persons of men and vessels of brass","Wheat and honey","Wine and wool","Purple and linen"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 27:13",verse:"Javan, Tubal, and Meshech, they were thy merchants: they traded the persons of men and vessels of brass in thy market.", insight:"They trafficked in human souls; Tyre's wealth had blood on its coins."},
{q:"What did the house of Togarmah trade?", options:["Horses and horsemen and mules","Sheep and goats","Ivory and ebony","Spices and gold"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 27:14",verse:"They of the house of Togarmah traded in thy fairs with horses and horsemen and mules.", insight:"Horses for her armies and mules for her caravans; even the cavalry trade could not outrun judgment."},
{q:"What did the men of Dedan bring?", options:["Horns of ivory and ebony","Chests of rich apparel","Bright iron and cassia","Lambs and goats"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 27:15",verse:"The men of Dedan were thy merchants; many isles were the merchandise of thine hand: they brought thee for a present horns of ivory and ebony.", insight:"Ivory and ebony from many isles; the ends of the earth paid her tribute."},
{q:"What did Judah and the land of Israel trade in her market?", options:["Wheat of Minnith, and Pannag, and honey, and oil, and balm","Silver and gold","Purple and fine linen","Horses and chariots"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 27:17",verse:"Judah, and the land of Israel, they were thy merchants: they traded in thy market wheat of Minnith, and Pannag, and honey, and oil, and balm.", insight:"God's own people traded in her market; even Israel fed the pride of Tyre."},
{q:"What did Damascus trade with her?", options:["The wine of Helbon, and white wool","Olive oil and figs","Copper and iron","Frankincense and myrrh"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 27:18",verse:"Damascus was thy merchant in the multitude of the wares of thy making, for the multitude of all riches; in the wine of Helbon, and white wool.", insight:"Helbon's wine and white wool; luxury upon luxury, until the cup of judgment was poured."},
{q:"What did the merchants of Sheba and Raamah bring?", options:["Chief of all spices, and all precious stones, and gold","Wheat and barley","Timber and pitch","Salt and fish"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 27:22",verse:"The merchants of Sheba and Raamah, they were thy merchants: they occupied in thy fairs with chief of all spices, and with all precious stones, and gold.", insight:"Spices, gems, and gold from Arabia; her market smelled of perfume and glittered with wealth."},
{q:"What did the ships of Tarshish do in her market?", options:["They did sing of her","They refused to trade","They burned her warehouses","They carried away her gold"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 27:25",verse:"The ships of Tarshish did sing of thee in thy market: and thou wast replenished, and made very glorious in the midst of the seas.", insight:"The great ships sang her praises; the world's applause is no anchor in a storm."},
{q:"What broke her in the midst of the seas?", options:["The east wind","A tidal wave","A sea monster","Her own heavy cargo"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 27:26",verse:"Thy rowers have brought thee into great waters: the east wind hath broken thee in the midst of the seas.", insight:"One east wind undid a perfect ship; God needs no army when He sends the weather."},
{q:"What do the mourners cry over Tyre?", options:["What city is like Tyrus, like the destroyed in the midst of the sea?","How are the mighty fallen in battle?","Where is the queen of the seas?","Who shall rebuild her walls?"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 27:32",verse:"And in their wailing they shall take up a lamentation for thee, and lament over thee, saying, What city is like Tyrus, like the destroyed in the midst of the sea?", insight:"No city like her, and no ruin like hers; uniqueness is no shield against the LORD."}
]},
{n:28, name:"The Prince and the King of Tyre", seal:{ref:"Ezekiel 28:15",verse:"Thou wast perfect in thy ways from the day that thou wast created, till iniquity was found in thee."}, facts:["Did you know the prince of Tyre claimed 'I am a God, I sit in the seat of God,' yet God answers with two short words: 'thou art a man'? It is one of the boldest claims and shortest answers in Scripture (Ezekiel 28:2).","Did you know the lament names nine precious stones as his covering, from sardius to carbuncle, and says his tabrets and pipes were prepared on the day he was created? Music and jewels were his from the beginning (Ezekiel 28:13).","Did you know the oracle against Tyre ends with hope for Israel? God will gather His people, and they shall dwell safely, build houses, and plant vineyards (Ezekiel 28:25 and 26)."], questions:[
{q:"What did the prince of Tyrus say in his heart?", options:["I am a God, I sit in the seat of God, in the midst of the seas","I am wiser than all kings","I have built this city by my might","No god can touch me"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 28:2",verse:"Son of man, say unto the prince of Tyrus, Thus saith the Lord GOD; Because thine heart is lifted up, and thou hast said, I am a God, I sit in the seat of God, in the midst of the seas; yet thou art a man, and not God, though thou set thine heart as the heart of God:", insight:"The throne of the sea became a throne in his mind; pride always mistakes the seat for the sitter."},
{q:"Though he set his heart as the heart of God, what was he really?", options:["A man, and not God","A god among men","An angel of light","A son of the sea"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 28:2",verse:"Son of man, say unto the prince of Tyrus, Thus saith the Lord GOD; Because thine heart is lifted up, and thou hast said, I am a God, I sit in the seat of God, in the midst of the seas; yet thou art a man, and not God, though thou set thine heart as the heart of God:", insight:"Yet thou art a man; the tallest claim in Scripture meets the shortest answer."},
{q:"Who was the prince of Tyre said to be wiser than?", options:["Daniel","Solomon","The wise men of Egypt","The elders of Gebal"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 28:3",verse:"Behold, thou art wiser than Daniel; there is no secret that they can hide from thee:", insight:"Wiser than Daniel, and still a fool; wisdom without worship is a sharpened sword in a blind hand."},
{q:"What had he gotten into his treasures by wisdom and understanding?", options:["Gold and silver","Ivory and ebony","Spices and gems","Horses and chariots"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 28:4",verse:"With thy wisdom and with thine understanding thou hast gotten thee riches, and hast gotten gold and silver into thy treasures:", insight:"Gold and silver filled his treasuries; riches gathered by wisdom still cannot buy humility."},
{q:"Why was his heart lifted up?", options:["Because of his riches","Because of his beauty","Because of his armies","Because of his age"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 28:5",verse:"By thy great wisdom and by thy traffick hast thou increased thy riches, and thine heart is lifted up because of thy riches:", insight:"Riches lifted what wisdom should have humbled; increase is a test most hearts fail."},
{q:"Whom will God bring upon him?", options:["Strangers, the terrible of the nations","The king of Egypt","The princes of the sea","The army of Israel"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 28:7",verse:"Behold, therefore I will bring strangers upon thee, the terrible of the nations: and they shall draw their swords against the beauty of thy wisdom, and they shall defile thy brightness.", insight:"The terrible of the nations; God answers pride with fear, the one language it understands."},
{q:"Where will they bring him down?", options:["To the pit","To the wilderness","To the house of bondage","To the farthest isle"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 28:8",verse:"They shall bring thee down to the pit, and thou shalt die the deaths of them that are slain in the midst of the seas.", insight:"Down to the pit with the slain; the seat of God is exchanged for a grave."},
{q:"Will he still say 'I am God' before him that slays him?", options:["No, he shall be a man, and no God, in the hand of him that slayeth him","Yes, he will boast to the end","He will say nothing at all","He will repent and be spared"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 28:9",verse:"Wilt thou yet say before him that slayeth thee, I am God? but thou shalt be a man, and no God, in the hand of him that slayeth thee.", insight:"Death strips every costume; in the slayer's hand, no one is a god."},
{q:"What death will he die?", options:["The deaths of the uncircumcised by the hand of strangers","The death of a king in his palace","A peaceful death in old age","The death of a martyr"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 28:10",verse:"Thou shalt die the deaths of the uncircumcised by the hand of strangers: for I have spoken it, saith the Lord GOD.", insight:"The deaths of the uncircumcised; the proud prince gets a stranger's grave."},
{q:"How is the king of Tyrus described in the lamentation?", options:["Thou sealest up the sum, full of wisdom, and perfect in beauty","A root out of dry ground","A bruised reed","A vessel of clay"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 28:12",verse:"Son of man, take up a lamentation upon the king of Tyrus, and say unto him, Thus saith the Lord GOD; Thou sealest up the sum, full of wisdom, and perfect in beauty.", insight:"Full of wisdom and perfect in beauty; the brightest lamps cast the darkest shadows when they fall."},
{q:"Where had he been?", options:["In Eden the garden of God","On the holy mountain","In the midst of the sea","In the land of Nod"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 28:13",verse:"Thou hast been in Eden the garden of God; every precious stone was thy covering, the sardius, topaz, and the diamond, the beryl, the onyx, and the jasper, the sapphire, the emerald, and the carbuncle, and gold: the workmanship of thy tabrets and of thy pipes was prepared in thee in the day that thou wast created.", insight:"Eden, the garden of God; the highest origin makes the deepest fall."},
{q:"Which of these was NOT named among the stones of his covering?", options:["The ruby","The sardius","The topaz","The carbuncle"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 28:13",verse:"Thou hast been in Eden the garden of God; every precious stone was thy covering, the sardius, topaz, and the diamond, the beryl, the onyx, and the jasper, the sapphire, the emerald, and the carbuncle, and gold: the workmanship of thy tabrets and of thy pipes was prepared in thee in the day that thou wast created.", insight:"Nine stones glittered on him, and gold besides; glory worn as jewelry becomes an idol."},
{q:"What was he?", options:["The anointed cherub that covereth","A seraph of the altar","A prince of Persia","A son of the morning"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 28:14",verse:"Thou art the anointed cherub that covereth; and I have set thee so: thou wast upon the holy mountain of God; thou hast walked up and down in the midst of the stones of fire.", insight:"The anointed cherub; set apart for the highest service, he fell to the lowest pride."},
{q:"Where had he walked up and down?", options:["In the midst of the stones of fire","In the courts of the temple","On the waves of the sea","In the garden of kings"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 28:14",verse:"Thou art the anointed cherub that covereth; and I have set thee so: thou wast upon the holy mountain of God; thou hast walked up and down in the midst of the stones of fire.", insight:"Stones of fire under his feet; he walked in glory and learned to love himself in it."},
{q:"By what was he filled with violence?", options:["By the multitude of his merchandise","By the strength of his armies","By the counsel of his princes","By the worship of his idols"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 28:16",verse:"By the multitude of thy merchandise they have filled the midst of thee with violence, and thou hast sinned: therefore I will cast thee as profane out of the mountain of God: and I will destroy thee, O covering cherub, from the midst of the stones of fire.", insight:"Trade filled him with violence; when profit is king, people become cargo."},
{q:"Why was his heart lifted up, and his wisdom corrupted?", options:["Because of his beauty and his brightness","Because of his riches","Because of his victories","Because of his age"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 28:17",verse:"Thine heart was lifted up because of thy beauty, thou hast corrupted thy wisdom by reason of thy brightness: I will cast thee to the ground, I will lay thee before kings, that they may behold thee.", insight:"Beauty lifted his heart and brightness corrupted his wisdom; the mirror is a dangerous teacher."},
{q:"What will God bring forth from the midst of him?", options:["A fire that shall devour him, bringing him to ashes","A sword that shall pierce him","A flood that shall drown him","A wind that shall scatter him"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 28:18",verse:"Thou hast defiled thy sanctuaries by the multitude of thine iniquities, by the iniquity of thy traffick; therefore will I bring forth a fire from the midst of thee, it shall devour thee, and I will bring thee to ashes upon the earth in the sight of all them that behold thee.", insight:"Fire from his own midst; the fuel of pride becomes the flame of judgment."},
{q:"What will all they that know him say?", options:["Thou shalt be a terror, and never shalt thou be any more","He was a great king","Let us rebuild his throne","His wisdom endures"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 28:19",verse:"All they that know thee among the people shall be astonished at thee: thou shalt be a terror, and never shalt thou be any more.", insight:"A terror, and never any more; that is the whole biography of pride."},
{q:"Against what city is Ezekiel told to set his face next?", options:["Zidon","Damascus","Ashkelon","Gaza"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 28:21",verse:"Son of man, set thy face against Zidon, and prophesy against it,", insight:"After Tyre, Zidon; God finishes with one proud city and turns to the next."},
{q:"What will the house of Israel do when God gathers them?", options:["Dwell safely, build houses, and plant vineyards","Return to Egypt","Build a new temple at Tyre","Scatter among the nations"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 28:26",verse:"And they shall dwell safely therein, and shall build houses, and plant vineyards; yea, they shall dwell with confidence, when I have executed judgments upon all those that despise them round about them; and they shall know that I am the LORD their God.", insight:"Houses built and vineyards planted; God's promise ends in a home, not a ruin."}
]},
{n:29, name:"Pharaoh, the Great Dragon", seal:{ref:"Ezekiel 29:3",verse:"Speak, and say, Thus saith the Lord GOD; Behold, I am against thee, Pharaoh king of Egypt, the great dragon that lieth in the midst of his rivers, which hath said, My river is mine own, and I have made it for myself."}, facts:["Did you know Pharaoh is pictured as the great dragon that lieth in the midst of his rivers, and God answers by putting hooks in his jaws? It is the Nile crocodile turned into a picture of a judged king (Ezekiel 29:3 and 4).","Did you know Egypt was to lie waste forty years and then return as the basest of the kingdoms? Judgment with a future: humbled, restored, and never again exalted over the nations (Ezekiel 29:11, 13, and 15).","Did you know Nebuchadrezzar's thirteen year siege of Tyre earned him no wages, so God paid him with Egypt? 'Because they wrought for me,' says the Lord (Ezekiel 29:18, 19, and 20)."], questions:[
{q:"When did the word of the LORD come against Pharaoh?", options:["In the tenth year, in the tenth month, in the twelfth day of the month","In the eleventh year, in the first day of the month","In the seven and twentieth year, in the first month","In the twelfth year, in the tenth month"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 29:1",verse:"In the tenth year, in the tenth month, in the twelfth day of the month, the word of the LORD came unto me, saying,", insight:"Dated to the day; God's word against empires keeps a calendar."},
{q:"Against whom is Ezekiel told to prophesy?", options:["Pharaoh king of Egypt, and against all Egypt","The king of Tyre","The princes of the sea","The elders of Israel"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 29:2",verse:"Son of man, set thy face against Pharaoh king of Egypt, and prophesy against him, and against all Egypt:", insight:"Against Pharaoh and all Egypt; when the head is proud, the whole body is judged."},
{q:"What does God call Pharaoh?", options:["The great dragon that lieth in the midst of his rivers","The lion of the desert","The eagle of the Nile","The serpent of the field"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 29:3",verse:"Speak, and say, Thus saith the Lord GOD; Behold, I am against thee, Pharaoh king of Egypt, the great dragon that lieth in the midst of his rivers, which hath said, My river is mine own, and I have made it for myself.", insight:"A dragon in his own rivers; power that wallows in its own waters is still a beast to God."},
{q:"What had Pharaoh said?", options:["My river is mine own, and I have made it for myself","I am a god forever","The Nile obeys my voice","No king is greater than I"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 29:3",verse:"Speak, and say, Thus saith the Lord GOD; Behold, I am against thee, Pharaoh king of Egypt, the great dragon that lieth in the midst of his rivers, which hath said, My river is mine own, and I have made it for myself.", insight:"'I have made it for myself'; the creature claiming the Creator's work is pride's oldest lie."},
{q:"What will God put in his jaws?", options:["Hooks","A bridle of iron","A bit and reins","Chains of brass"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 29:4",verse:"But I will put hooks in thy jaws, and I will cause the fish of thy rivers to stick unto thy scales, and I will bring thee up out of the midst of thy rivers, and all the fish of thy rivers shall stick unto thy scales.", insight:"Hooks in the dragon's jaws; God fishes for monsters and lands them."},
{q:"Where will God leave him thrown?", options:["Into the wilderness, upon the open fields","Into the depths of the sea","Into the land of Canaan","Into the pit of hell"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 29:5",verse:"And I will leave thee thrown into the wilderness, thee and all the fish of thy rivers: thou shalt fall upon the open fields; thou shalt not be brought together, nor gathered: I have given thee for meat to the beasts of the field and to the fowls of the heaven.", insight:"The river god ends in the wilderness; God moves His catch far from its element."},
{q:"What had Egypt been to the house of Israel?", options:["A staff of reed","A wall of brass","A shield of gold","A tower of strength"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 29:6",verse:"And all the inhabitants of Egypt shall know that I am the LORD, because they have been a staff of reed to the house of Israel.", insight:"A staff of reed; lean on Egypt and it pierces your hand."},
{q:"What happened when Israel leaned on Egypt?", options:["She broke, and rent all their shoulder","She bore them up safely","She led them to victory","She paid their debts"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 29:7",verse:"When they took hold of thee by thy hand, thou didst break, and rend all their shoulder: and when they leaned upon thee, thou brakest, and madest all their loins to be at a stand.", insight:"She broke and rent their shoulder; false supports wound the very hands that trust them."},
{q:"What will God bring upon Egypt?", options:["A sword, and cut off man and beast out of her","A famine of seven years","A plague of locusts","Darkness for three days"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 29:8",verse:"Therefore thus saith the Lord GOD; Behold, I will bring a sword upon thee, and cut off man and beast out of thee.", insight:"A sword that cuts off man and beast; no life in Egypt is outside God's reach."},
{q:"From where to where will Egypt be made waste?", options:["From the tower of Syene even unto the border of Ethiopia","From the Nile to the Red Sea","From Memphis to Thebes","From Goshen to the wilderness"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 29:10",verse:"Behold, therefore I am against thee, and against thy rivers, and I will make the land of Egypt utterly waste and desolate, from the tower of Syene even unto the border of Ethiopia.", insight:"From Syene to Ethiopia, the whole length of the land; judgment covers what pride claimed."},
{q:"How long shall Egypt be uninhabited?", options:["Forty years","Seventy years","Seven years","A hundred years"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 29:11",verse:"No foot of man shall pass through it, nor foot of beast shall pass through it, neither shall it be inhabited forty years.", insight:"Forty years without man or beast; God can empty a nation and keep the receipt."},
{q:"What will happen to the Egyptians?", options:["They shall be scattered among the nations and dispersed through the countries","They shall be carried to Babylon alone","They shall flee to the isles","They shall hide in the wilderness"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 29:12",verse:"And I will make the land of Egypt desolate in the midst of the countries that are desolate, and her cities among the cities that are laid waste shall be desolate forty years: and I will scatter the Egyptians among the nations, and will disperse them through the countries.", insight:"Scattered and dispersed; the river people spread like chaff on foreign winds."},
{q:"When will God gather the Egyptians?", options:["At the end of forty years","After seventy years","In the third generation","When the Nile fails"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 29:13",verse:"Yet thus saith the Lord GOD; At the end of forty years will I gather the Egyptians from the people whither they were scattered:", insight:"At the end of forty years; judgment has a finish line, and mercy is waiting there."},
{q:"Where will they return?", options:["Into the land of Pathros, the land of their habitation","Into the land of Goshen","Into the city of Noph","Into the wilderness of Sinai"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 29:14",verse:"And I will bring again the captivity of Egypt, and will cause them to return into the land of Pathros, into the land of their habitation; and they shall be there a base kingdom.", insight:"Back to Pathros, their old home; restoration is real, even for a judged nation."},
{q:"What kind of kingdom will Egypt be?", options:["The basest of the kingdoms","A mighty empire again","A kingdom of priests","A wealthy trading nation"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 29:15",verse:"It shall be the basest of the kingdoms; neither shall it exalt itself any more above the nations: for I will diminish them, that they shall no more rule over the nations.", insight:"The basest of the kingdoms; God shrinks the proud to keep them humble."},
{q:"When did the word come about Nebuchadrezzar's wages?", options:["In the seven and twentieth year, in the first month, in the first day of the month","In the tenth year, in the tenth month","In the eleventh year, in the first month","In the twelfth year, in the tenth month"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 29:17",verse:"And it came to pass in the seven and twentieth year, in the first month, in the first day of the month, the word of the LORD came unto me, saying,", insight:"Seven and twenty years into the prophecy; God's books stay open until the account is settled."},
{q:"What did Nebuchadrezzar's army suffer at Tyre?", options:["Every head was made bald, and every shoulder was peeled","They lost half their number","Their ships were burned","Their king was wounded"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 29:18",verse:"Son of man, Nebuchadrezzar king of Babylon caused his army to serve a great service against Tyrus: every head was made bald, and every shoulder was peeled: yet had he no wages, nor his army, for Tyrus, for the service that he had served against it:", insight:"Bald heads and peeled shoulders; the siege of Tyre cost Babylon dearly."},
{q:"What wages had he and his army for Tyre?", options:["None; yet had he no wages for the service","The gold of Tyre","The ships of the harbor","The daughters of the city"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 29:18",verse:"Son of man, Nebuchadrezzar king of Babylon caused his army to serve a great service against Tyrus: every head was made bald, and every shoulder was peeled: yet had he no wages, nor his army, for Tyrus, for the service that he had served against it:", insight:"No wages for thirteen years of war; even pagan labor deserves its pay, and God pays it."},
{q:"What will God give Nebuchadrezzar as wages?", options:["The land of Egypt, her multitude, her spoil, and her prey","The city of Tyre","The isles of the sea","The treasures of Jerusalem"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 29:19",verse:"Therefore thus saith the Lord GOD; Behold, I will give the land of Egypt unto Nebuchadrezzar king of Babylon; and he shall take her multitude, and take her spoil, and take her prey; and it shall be the wages for his army.", insight:"Egypt as wages; God pays His servants with the wealth of nations."},
{q:"What will God cause to bud forth in that day?", options:["The horn of the house of Israel","The vine of Judah","The cedar of Lebanon","The lily of the field"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 29:21",verse:"In that day will I cause the horn of the house of Israel to bud forth, and I will give thee the opening of the mouth in the midst of them; and they shall know that I am the LORD.", insight:"The horn of Israel buds while Egypt withers; God's people rise as empires fall."}
]},
{n:30, name:"Woe Worth the Day", seal:{ref:"Ezekiel 30:8",verse:"And they shall know that I am the LORD, when I have set a fire in Egypt, and when all her helpers shall be destroyed."}, facts:["Did you know Ezekiel opens this oracle with 'Howl ye, Woe worth the day'? The day of the LORD is described as a cloudy day, the time of the heathen, when God judges the nations (Ezekiel 30:2 and 3).","Did you know God's judgment walks through Egypt city by city? Noph, Pathros, Zoan, No, Sin, Aven, Pi-beseth, and Tehaphnehes are each named for their share of the sentence (Ezekiel 30:13, 14, 15, 16, 17, and 18).","Did you know Pharaoh's broken arm could not be bound up, so God broke both arms and strengthened Babylon's instead? The groaning of the dragon closes the chapter (Ezekiel 30:21, 22, and 24)."], questions:[
{q:"What cry opens the prophecy?", options:["Howl ye, Woe worth the day","Sing ye, for the day is come","Flee ye to the mountains","Be still, and know"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 30:2",verse:"Son of man, prophesy and say, Thus saith the Lord GOD; Howl ye, Woe worth the day", insight:"Howl ye; some days are for weeping, and the wise know which ones."},
{q:"What kind of day is the day of the LORD?", options:["A cloudy day; it shall be the time of the heathen","A day of bright sunshine","A day of feasting","A day of rest"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 30:3",verse:"For the day is near, even the day of the LORD is near, a cloudy day; it shall be the time of the heathen.", insight:"A cloudy day; God's day dawns dark for the nations that forgot Him."},
{q:"Where will great pain be when the sword comes upon Egypt?", options:["In Ethiopia","In Libya","In Lydia","In Chub"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 30:4",verse:"And the sword shall come upon Egypt, and great pain shall be in Ethiopia, when the slain shall fall in Egypt, and they shall take away her multitude, and her foundations shall be broken down.", insight:"Pain in Ethiopia when Egypt falls; nations are tangled together, and one fall shakes many."},
{q:"Which of these shall fall with Egypt by the sword?", options:["Ethiopia, and Libya, and Lydia","Persia and Media","Judah and Israel","Tyre and Zidon"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 30:5",verse:"Ethiopia, and Libya, and Lydia, and all the mingled people, and Chub, and the men of the land that is in league, shall fall with them by the sword.", insight:"Allies fall together; shared sin means a shared sentence."},
{q:"From where shall they fall by the sword?", options:["From the tower of Syene","From the walls of Noph","From the gates of No","From the river of Egypt"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 30:6",verse:"Thus saith the LORD; They also that uphold Egypt shall fall; and the pride of her power shall come down: from the tower of Syene shall they fall in it by the sword, saith the Lord GOD.", insight:"From Syene's tower; the pride of her power comes down at the border."},
{q:"When will they know that He is the LORD?", options:["When He has set a fire in Egypt","When the Nile overflows","When Pharaoh repents","When the idols speak"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 30:8",verse:"And they shall know that I am the LORD, when I have set a fire in Egypt, and when all her helpers shall be destroyed.", insight:"Fire in Egypt teaches what sermons could not; judgment is God's loudest pulpit."},
{q:"How will the messengers go forth to the Ethiopians?", options:["In ships","On horseback","On foot","By chariot"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 30:9",verse:"In that day shall messengers go forth from me in ships to make the careless Ethiopians afraid, and great pain shall come upon them, as in the day of Egypt: for, lo, it cometh.", insight:"Messengers in ships to the careless; God sends warnings even to the comfortable."},
{q:"By whose hand will God make the multitude of Egypt to cease?", options:["By the hand of Nebuchadrezzar king of Babylon","By the hand of Pharaoh","By the hand of the Ethiopians","By the hand of the Libyans"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 30:10",verse:"Thus saith the Lord GOD; I will also make the multitude of Egypt to cease by the hand of Nebuchadrezzar king of Babylon.", insight:"Babylon's hand does God's work; the Judge uses whatever gavel He chooses."},
{q:"How are Nebuchadrezzar and his people described?", options:["The terrible of the nations","The mighty of the east","The swift of foot","The wise in counsel"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 30:11",verse:"He and his people with him, the terrible of the nations, shall be brought to destroy the land: and they shall draw their swords against Egypt, and fill the land with the slain.", insight:"The terrible of the nations; God is not embarrassed to use fearsome instruments."},
{q:"What will God do to the rivers?", options:["Make them dry","Turn them to blood","Fill them with fish","Divide them in two"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 30:12",verse:"And I will make the rivers dry, and sell the land into the hand of the wicked: and I will make the land waste, and all that is therein, by the hand of strangers: I the LORD have spoken it.", insight:"Dry rivers; the god of the Nile watches his own waters fail."},
{q:"What will happen to the idols and images?", options:["God will destroy the idols, and cause the images to cease out of Noph","They shall be carried to Babylon","They shall be hidden in the desert","They shall be worshipped forever"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 30:13",verse:"Thus saith the Lord GOD; I will also destroy the idols, and I will cause their images to cease out of Noph; and there shall be no more a prince of the land of Egypt: and I will put a fear in the land of Egypt.", insight:"Idols destroyed, images ceased; Egypt's gods cannot save Egypt."},
{q:"What will there be no more of in Egypt?", options:["A prince of the land of Egypt","A harvest of wheat","A ship on the Nile","A song in the temple"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 30:13",verse:"Thus saith the Lord GOD; I will also destroy the idols, and I will cause their images to cease out of Noph; and there shall be no more a prince of the land of Egypt: and I will put a fear in the land of Egypt.", insight:"No more a prince; the throne itself is removed when God judges."},
{q:"In which cities will God execute judgments?", options:["Zoan and No","Noph and Sin","Aven and Pi-beseth","Syene and Tehaphnehes"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 30:14",verse:"And I will make Pathros desolate, and will set fire in Zoan, and will execute judgments in No.", insight:"Fire in Zoan, judgments in No; God calls the cities by name."},
{q:"Upon what will God pour His fury?", options:["Sin, the strength of Egypt","Noph, the city of idols","No, the ancient city","Zoan, the royal city"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 30:15",verse:"And I will pour my fury upon Sin, the strength of Egypt; and I will cut off the multitude of No.", insight:"Sin, the strength of Egypt; God strikes where a nation feels strongest."},
{q:"What will happen to No and Noph?", options:["No shall be rent asunder, and Noph shall have distresses daily","They shall be rebuilt in glory","They shall be spared for their repentance","They shall flee to the wilderness"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 30:16",verse:"And I will set fire in Egypt: Sin shall have great pain, and No shall be rent asunder, and Noph shall have distresses daily.", insight:"Rent asunder and daily distress; judgment can be both sudden and slow."},
{q:"Who shall fall by the sword?", options:["The young men of Aven and of Pi-beseth","The old men of Noph","The priests of Sin","The sailors of the Nile"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 30:17",verse:"The young men of Aven and of Pi-beseth shall fall by the sword: and these cities shall go into captivity.", insight:"The young men fall first; war harvests the future before the past."},
{q:"At what place shall the day be darkened?", options:["Tehaphnehes","Syene","Pathros","Memphis"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 30:18",verse:"At Tehaphnehes also the day shall be darkened, when I shall break there the yokes of Egypt: and the pomp of her strength shall cease in her: as for her, a cloud shall cover her, and her daughters shall go into captivity.", insight:"Darkness at Tehaphnehes; the yokes break where the pomp of strength once paraded."},
{q:"When did the word come about Pharaoh's broken arm?", options:["In the eleventh year, in the first month, in the seventh day of the month","In the tenth year, in the tenth month","In the seven and twentieth year","In the twelfth year, in the tenth month"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 30:20",verse:"And it came to pass in the eleventh year, in the first month, in the seventh day of the month, that the word of the LORD came unto me, saying,", insight:"Another dated word; God keeps a diary of every broken arm."},
{q:"What had happened to Pharaoh's arm?", options:["God had broken it, and it shall not be bound up to be healed","It was wounded in battle","It was withered by disease","It was strong as iron"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 30:21",verse:"Son of man, I have broken the arm of Pharaoh king of Egypt; and, lo, it shall not be bound up to be healed, to put a roller to bind it, to make it strong to hold the sword.", insight:"Broken and never bound; some wounds are judgments, not accidents."},
{q:"What will Pharaoh do before the king of Babylon?", options:["Groan with the groanings of a deadly wounded man","Bow and pay tribute","Flee to Ethiopia","Sue for peace"], a:0, ref:"Ezekiel 30:24",verse:"And I will strengthen the arms of the king of Babylon, and put my sword in his hand: but I will break Pharaoh’s arms, and he shall groan before him with the groanings of a deadly wounded man.", insight:"The dragon groans like a dying man; pride's end is always a whimper, never a roar."}
]}
]);
